WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:06.019
In 1980 the great economist Milton Friedman
published “Free to Choose”.

00:00:06.019 --> 00:00:11.160
In it, he extolled the virtues of free-market
Capitalism — its ability to lift hard-working

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:16.369
families out of poverty, modernize entire
economies, and raise the standard of living

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:17.900
for everyone.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:22.839
The United States, he and his wife explained,
was among the finest examples of what’s

00:00:22.839 --> 00:00:27.079
possible when a government simply takes its
hand off the wheel.

00:00:27.079 --> 00:00:33.239
His Utopia, however, was not the U.S., but
rather somewhere halfway across the globe

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:39.820
— Hong Kong — quote “the modern exemplar
of free markets and limited government”.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:45.039
So enamored was he with its faithful application
of Capitalism that Friedman devoted many of

00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:49.899
his remaining years delivering talks, writing
articles, and traveling the world telling

00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:50.899
its story.

00:00:50.899 --> 00:01:01.469
It 

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:08.840
was such a textbook rags-to-riches example
in part thanks to its physical transformation…

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:14.550
This is Victoria Harbor around 1890 — a
barren coastline the British government was

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:19.190
once indifferent to,
Here it is in the early 1960s,

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:25.770
And finally, this is Hong Kong, in all its
vertical glory, at the end of the twentieth

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:26.770
century.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:34.340
Today, with 70% more skyscrapers than New
York, it just exudes abundance.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:40.190
Its GDP per capita — already formidable
on its own — becomes nothing short of miraculous

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:43.719
when compared to its once-colonial master.

00:01:43.719 --> 00:01:49.050
And while the U.S. is more closely associated
with Capitalism thanks to its size, in some

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:54.789
ways it more closely resembles Finland, Sweden,
or Denmark than Hong Kong.

00:01:54.789 --> 00:01:59.670
According to the Fraser Institute and Heritage
Foundation, there’s no place on earth with

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:06.719
more private ownership, lower public expenditure,
simpler tax law, or less regulation.

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:09.739
Its leader is even called the Chief Executive.

00:02:09.739 --> 00:02:16.319
The very first of which was, quite fittingly,
Tung Chee-hwa, the billionaire CEO of a shipping

00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:17.849
company.

00:02:17.849 --> 00:02:23.271
But what appears to be an almost religious
adherence to Capitalism may have been, in

00:02:23.271 --> 00:02:26.590
fact, more historical happenstance.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:30.720
The great economist may have been wrong all
along.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.349
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and Nebula — where
you can watch the bonus video which accompanies

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:40.540
this one for just $15 bucks a year.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:45.700
During the British Empire, colonial bureaucrats
were not sent directly from London, as you

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.300
might expect, but rather hopped between Crown
Colonies.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:55.269
Sir Denys Roberts, for example, began his
career in the Army, before being posted to

00:02:55.269 --> 00:03:00.920
Nyasaland, a British protectorate in Africa,
followed by Gibraltar, and then as the Chief

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.299
Justice of Hong Kong.

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:08.300
Because of this, by the time the sun finally
set on the Empire with Hong Kong as the last

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:13.330
major holdout, there had developed a great
difference in style and philosophy between

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:16.430
London and its distant colonies.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:20.130
Milton Friedman called this an “economic
experiment”.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:25.880
Britain and Hong Kong — although both “British”
in name — pursued two very different economic

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:30.160
systems: one hands-on and the other, off.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:36.180
The reason, in the free-market narrative,
was a man named Sir John James Cowperthwaite.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:40.379
Cowperthwaite was the Financial Secretary
during the 60s.

00:03:40.379 --> 00:03:44.860
He believed government intervention did more
harm than good; and, in any case, was too

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:46.689
hard to predict.

00:03:46.689 --> 00:03:51.830
He accidentally became a kind of Capitalist
hero after Friedman met him during a brief

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:53.470
stopover.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:58.151
When asked about the local economy, Cowperthwaite
replied that he honestly didn’t know the

00:03:58.151 --> 00:04:02.799
answer — he was philosophically opposed
to keeping any records as it might give the

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:05.379
government grounds to “interfere”.

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:12.329
His successor gave this do-nothing approach
an academic-sounding name: “Positive Non-Interventionism”

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:14.800
and the rest is history.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.290
The true story, however, is quite different.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:23.410
The first reason Hong Kong was so financially
prudent was that it had to be.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:28.520
Britain expected that quote “the local revenue
will be adequate to defray . . . all the . . . expenses

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.750
of the government of Hong Kong.”

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:36.550
London even made the city pay for most of
the British Garrison — a giant, intimidating

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:40.260
30-story military base in the middle of the
city's financial district.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:45.660
Second, with financial independence came administrative
independence.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:50.790
The British elite in Hong Kong had no desire
to cede control to London, who would surely

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.590
attach conditions to further funding.

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:58.330
By spending so little, the colony remained
self-sufficient and the rich could enjoy their

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.120
autonomy, without needing to beg the Queen
for help.

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:07.210
They also worried generous social welfare
would attract too many migrants from up North.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:15.540
And so, between 1948 and 97, Hong Kong ran
a deficit for just seven of fifty years.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:21.360
Finally, and most disturbingly, free-market
ideology both distracted from Britain’s

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.570
failures and justified its ethnic discrimination.

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:30.280
Contrary to what many believe, Hong Kong was
never democratic.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:35.780
Bureaucrats were appointed from 5,000 miles
away, and never by popular vote.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:40.870
The supposed meritocracy of the “invisible
hand” of the free-market was a convenient

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.130
scapegoat.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:46.640
It also provided the colonial government with
some source of legitimacy.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:52.300
It was supposed to be “above” politics,
composed merely of neutral technicians allowing

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:54.420
citizens to prosper.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:06:01.470
In reality, zoning laws, elite schools, and
favoritism all gave the 1% British population

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.080
an unfair advantage.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:11.460
As you can see, Capitalist policies were driven
less by ideology and more by historical circumstances.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:16.940
And while its lack of natural resources is
often cited as further evidence of its miraculous

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:23.310
transformation, this argument relies on a
very narrow definition of “natural resources”

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:28.800
Is strategically-located geography like that
of Hong Kong or Singapore not the best and

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.860
least corruptible of resources?

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:37.660
But regardless of why it adopted the policies
that it did, the Hong Kong of today is either

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:44.820
not an accurate example of Capitalism in practice,
or a striking example of its perils.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:47.680
The list of monopolies is long…

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:53.220
- At its one and only container port, the
world’s 8th largest, just two companies:

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:59.860
Hutchinson and Modern together control 21
of its 24 berths — 87%.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:05.690
- The privately-owned Jockey Club is granted
a total monopoly on gambling, which, in exchange,

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:10.200
sprays charity around the city at a rate only
guilt could explain.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:14.910
- The government carefully regulates both
the number of public buses and bus companies

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:20.710
— resulting in only two major players: KMB
and New World First Bus.

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:25.960
- There are just two electric companies: China
Light and Power, and HongKong Electric, which

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.040
serve different areas.

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:33.650
- In a city of nearly 8 million, every last
pound of fresh beef comes from one private

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:39.910
company: Ng Fung Hong — who has a complete
monopoly on the license to import cattle.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:43.580
- Likewise, consumers have only one source
of gas.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:49.870
But all this pales in comparison to the hidden
retail duopoly.

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:55.460
Just two conglomerates, through a tangled
mess of subsidiaries, own so many retail stores

00:07:55.460 --> 00:08:00.770
that it would be nearly impossible for someone
living in Hong Kong not to patronize at least

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:01.810
one.

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:07.740
On one side is Jardine Matheson, which owns
Dairy Farm International, which owns every

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:14.750
local Mannings, IKEA, Wellcome, Pizza Hut,
Market Place by Jasons, Starbucks, KFC, Maxim’s,

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:18.530
and the city’s 900 7-Eleven’s.

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:25.940
On the other side is CK Hutchison Holdings,
founded by Hong Kong’s richest man, Li Ka-shing.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:27.660
Hutchinson owns A.S.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:34.450
Watson Group, which owns Three, Fusion, Watsons,
PARKnSHOP, TaSTE, and Fortress.

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:39.680
Watsons and Mannings control nearly the entire
personal care market, while PARKnSHOP and

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:43.060
Wellcome are the two largest grocery stores.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:48.320
It should be no surprise, then, that groceries
are more expensive in Hong Kong than Tokyo,

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.830
Singapore, or the UK.

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:53.950
And then there are the hidden taxes.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:59.550
While Hong Kong has no capital gains, sales,
withholding, or value-added tax, it charges

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:03.360
a very high stamp duty on homes.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:08.220
Locals pay a standard 15% tax on homes after
their first.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:13.750
Non-permanent residents pay 30% — effectively
preventing any foreigner from ever buying

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:14.820
a home.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:20.310
But at least the government gets out of the
way, letting the free market run its course,

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:21.320
right?

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:26.780
How else could it be ranked the most economically
free place on earth, for decades?

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:31.710
To answer this question, one need only look
to the Food Truck Pilot Scheme.

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:36.920
In 2015, the Hong Kong government decided
food trucks, at this point already popular

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:41.260
in cities around the world, might attract
tourists.

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:47.610
After 9 months of “study” and one year
of “preparation”, a Pilot Scheme was launched.

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:51.670
Only eight trucks would be allowed for a city
of eight million.

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:57.010
Then there were the rules: Kitchens needed
to be at least 65 square feet, trucks required

00:09:57.010 --> 00:10:02.930
a 32-gallon water tank, and the sink had to
be 1.5 feet long, to name just a few.

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:09.300
To meet these requirements, restaurant owners
had to invest in entirely new and costly vehicles.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:14.900
Changing any part of the menu required approval
from the government, and no tables, chairs,

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:16.480
or tents were allowed.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:19.180
But, they did make an app!

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:24.170
It conveniently shows the location of each
food truck — oh except they aren’t allowed

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:25.370
to move.

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:31.020
The trucks must stay in the same location
and pay no less than 15% of gross revenue

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:33.040
on parking.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:38.220
Ironically, food trucks are perhaps the best
illustration of free-market Capitalism — the

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:43.360
bad quickly fail while the good remain, chefs
are free to experiment with new dishes with

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:46.560
low risk, and there are few barriers to entry.

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:50.850
A perfect competitive environment that benefits
the consumer.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:53.950
So, why so much regulation?

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:58.810
What explains Hong Kong’s laissez-faire
attitude when it comes to groceries, utilities,

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:03.410
and busses, and yet its micromanaging of food
trucks?

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:09.470
In reality, its bias is not in the direction
of free-markets, or non-intervention in general,

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:15.490
but in maintaining the status quo — one
that benefits the largest corporations.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:21.170
Hong Kong appears to be a free-market paradise
because it allows large corporations free

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:25.520
reign, until only those large corporations
remain.

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:30.520
But wherever there are small businesses — food
trucks, for example — it suddenly doesn’t

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:33.290
seem so ideologically strict.

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:37.350
The only pattern to its behavior is pro-business:

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:42.640
One third of employees work more than 48 hours
a week, making it the most “overworked”

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:43.970
city on earth.

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:49.870
It has a low minimum wage, and some CEOs are
even allowed to bypass its COVID travel quarantine

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:52.070
— one of the strictest in the world.

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:58.000
A more accurate word might be “corporatocracy”
— rule by corporation.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.550
And there’s a very good reason for this.

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:06.460
In any country, corporations are afforded
some of the same rights as we are: the ability

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:10.450
to sign contracts and sue others in court,
for example.

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:14.500
Some countries even describe corporations
as legal “people”.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:18.100
But in Hong Kong, corporations actually vote.

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:21.990
That’s not hyperbole for political influence.

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:26.090
No, corporations legally, cast ballots.

00:12:26.090 --> 00:12:31.390
The Hong Kong election system is deliberately
complicated and difficult to understand, which

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:37.310
is why I explain How Corporations Vote in
Hong Kong in a separate, unaffected-by-the-Youtube-algorithm

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:39.190
video on Nebula.

